1.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: My current belief remains **A. overestimate**, under the standard population-average interpretation of “per-exposure vaccine efficacy.” For an all-or-nothing vaccine, let \(p\) be the protected fraction; the average per-exposure VE across vaccinees is \(p\). With an incidence rate ratio, protected vaccinees contribute infection-free person-time, diluting the vaccinated rate. With hazard \(\lambda\) and follow-up \(T\),  
\[
\text{IRR}
=
\frac{(1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})}
{p\lambda T+(1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})}
< 1-p
\]
because \(\lambda T > 1-e^{-\lambda T}\). Thus \(1-\text{IRR} > p\), so it overestimates the average per-exposure VE.

Current public tally: A = Colleagues 1, 2, 3, 5; B = Colleagues 4, 6; C = none. Colleague 4 revised from C to B; Colleague 5 shifted from C to A; Colleague 1 shifted from B to A. The main unresolved issue is the estimand. C is correct only for a cumulative risk ratio or rare-infection/short-follow-up approximation where person-time dilution is negligible. B is plausible only if “per-exposure VE” is defined conditionally among protected responders, i.e. 100% efficacy for those individuals, rather than the usual marginal/average per-exposure VE. Given the wording “incidence rate ratio” and the standard population-average interpretation, I continue to favor **A**.

