5.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: I provisionally maintain **A. overestimate**, under the standard population-average interpretation of “per-exposure vaccine efficacy” for an all-or-nothing vaccine. Let \(p\) be the protected fraction; then the marginal per-exposure VE is \(p\). With hazard \(\lambda\) and follow-up \(T\),

\[
\text{IRR}
=
\frac{(1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})}
{p\lambda T+(1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})}
< 1-p,
\]

so \(1-\text{IRR}>p\). The protected vaccinees contribute infection-free person-time, diluting the vaccinated incidence rate. **C** would apply only to a cumulative risk ratio or a rare-infection/short-follow-up approximation. **B** applies only if “per-exposure VE” is redefined as the conditional efficacy among protected responders, which is \(1\), not the usual marginal estimand.

Current round 3 public positions: A = Colleagues 1, 2, 3, 5; B = Colleagues 4, 6. The disagreement is definitional, not mathematical: C4/C6 believe “all-or-nothing” plus “per-exposure” points to the conditional 100% effect among protected responders; the A side treats the estimand as the population-average protected fraction \(p\). The algebra is accepted by both sides. Given the explicit wording “incidence rate ratio” and standard vaccine-efficacy framework, I still favor **A**, but the unresolved estimand ambiguity remains the main risk.

