2.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: I continue to favor A. overestimate under the standard population-average interpretation of “per-exposure vaccine efficacy” for an all-or-nothing vaccine. Let \(p\) be the protected fraction; the marginal per-exposure VE across all vaccinees is \(p\) (protected have efficacy 1, unprotected have 0). With unvaccinated hazard \(\lambda\) and follow-up \(T\), vaccinated events are \((1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})\) and vaccinated person-time is \(pT+(1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})/\lambda\), so
\[
\text{IRR}
=
\frac{(1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})}
{\lambda pT+(1-p)(1-e^{-\lambda T})}
<1-p.
\]
Thus \(1-\text{IRR}>p\). The overestimate occurs because completely protected vaccinees contribute infection-free person-time, diluting the vaccinated incidence rate. C would apply only to a cumulative risk ratio or a rare/short-follow-up approximation, not generally to an incidence rate ratio. B remains a coherent rival if “per-exposure vaccine efficacy” is read as the conditional all-or-none effect among protected responders, which is \(1\); then \(1-\text{IRR}<1\) underestimates it. Colleagues 4 and 6 press this estimand-based B argument. Current majority favors A (C1, C2, C3, C5), with C4 and C6 favoring B. Unresolved issue: whether the wording “all-or-nothing” is intended to force the conditional 100% protection estimand. Unless that is clarified, I maintain A as the best answer under the usual population-average vaccine-efficacy framework.

